<h2>Richardson homes, offices, and common claim issues</h2>
<h3>Mid-century ranch homes</h3>
<p>Many older Richardson neighborhoods are filled with single-story brick ranch homes on mature, tree-shaded lots. Long, low roof lines with composition shingles take hail across a large surface, and big trees create wind and limb-impact risk. Older roofs often draw heavy depreciation, and repairs can trigger code items such as new underlayment or ventilation. Our <a href="/services/roof-damage-insurance-claims/">roof damage claims</a> and <a href="/services/hail-damage-claims/">hail damage claims</a> pages explain what to document.</p>
<h3>Newer homes and multifamily</h3>
<p>Two-story homes and apartment communities bring steeper roofs, more siding and window exposure, and multiple units under one policy. Wind-driven rain through damaged siding or windows can lead to interior <a href="/services/water-damage-claims/">water damage claims</a> that are easy to under-scope.</p>
<h3>Office and commercial buildings</h3>
<p>Low-slope TPO and modified-bitumen roofs, rooftop HVAC units, and large glass facades are common along the business corridors. Hail and wind damage to these systems can be hard to see from the ground, and business interruption terms need a close read. See <a href="/services/commercial-property-claims/">commercial property claims</a>.</p>

